{"name":"Little Mermaid","hex":"#2d454a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"little","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Small, not large, limited, particularly:","Small, limited in size."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Not much.","Not at all."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/little"},{"term":"mermaid","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mythological creature with a woman's head and upper body, and a tail of a fish.","Coloured a brilliant turquoise."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mermaid"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Little Mermaid","blocks":[{"text":"\"The Little Mermaid\", sometimes translated in English as \"The Little Sea Maid\", is a literary fairy tale written by Danish author Hans Christian Andersen. Originally published in 1837 as part of a collection of fairy tales for children, the story follows the journey of a young mermaid princess who is willing to give up her life in the sea as a mermaid to gain a human soul."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Edmund_Dulac_-_The_Mermaid_-_The_Prince.jpg/330px-Edmund_Dulac_-_The_Mermaid_-_The_Prince.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Little_Mermaid","wikidata":"Q11859"},{"title":"Mermaid","blocks":[{"text":"In folklore, a mermaid is an aquatic creature with the head and upper body of a female human and the tail of a fish. Mermaids appear in the folklore of many cultures worldwide, including Europe, Latin America, Asia, and Africa."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/John_William_Waterhouse_A_Mermaid.jpg/330px-John_William_Waterhouse_A_Mermaid.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mermaid","wikidata":"Q182559"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Page_139_illustration_in_fairy_